<!-- doc/src/sgml/pqc.sgml -->
<chapter id="pqc">
 <title>FortressQL: Post-Quantum Cryptography</title>

 <indexterm zone="pqc">
  <primary>post-quantum cryptography</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  FortressQL extends PostgreSQL with comprehensive post-quantum cryptography
  (PQC) support across all layers: TLS transport, authentication, and
  application-level encryption.  This protects against the "harvest now,
  decrypt later" threat where data encrypted today with classical algorithms
  can be stored and decrypted once quantum computers mature.
 </para>

 <para>
  FortressQL integrates the NIST-standardized PQC algorithms:
 </para>

 <itemizedlist>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <emphasis>ML-KEM</emphasis> (FIPS 203) &mdash; Module-Lattice Key
    Encapsulation Mechanism, for key exchange (ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768,
    ML-KEM-1024).
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <emphasis>ML-DSA</emphasis> (FIPS 204) &mdash; Module-Lattice Digital
    Signature Algorithm (ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65, ML-DSA-87).
   </para>
  </listitem>
  <listitem>
   <para>
    <emphasis>SLH-DSA</emphasis> (FIPS 205) &mdash; Stateless Hash-Based
    Digital Signature Algorithm (12 variants with SHA-2 and SHAKE).
   </para>
  </listitem>
 </itemizedlist>

 <sect1 id="pqc-build">
  <title>Building with PQC Support</title>

  <para>
   PQC support requires <productname>liboqs</productname> (Open Quantum Safe)
   and <productname>OpenSSL</productname> 3.0 or later.
  </para>

  <para>
   To enable PQC support:
  </para>

<programlisting>
meson setup build -Dssl=openssl -Dpqc=enabled
meson compile -C build
</programlisting>

  <para>
   The <literal>-Dpqc=auto</literal> option (default) enables PQC if liboqs
   is found.  Use <literal>-Dpqc=disabled</literal> to build without PQC
   support, producing a binary identical to vanilla PostgreSQL.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pqc-tls">
  <title>PQC TLS Transport</title>

  <para>
   FortressQL supports hybrid PQC key exchange using OpenSSL's group
   negotiation.  This works with both OpenSSL 3.5+ (native PQC) and
   older versions with the OQS provider.
  </para>

  <para>
   The following GUC parameters control PQC TLS:
  </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><varname>ssl_pqc_mode</varname> (<type>enum</type>)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Controls PQC key exchange mode.  Values: <literal>off</literal>
      (classical only), <literal>hybrid</literal> (default, PQC + classical),
      <literal>pqc-only</literal> (PQC algorithms only).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><varname>ssl_pqc_groups</varname> (<type>string</type>)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Colon-separated list of key exchange groups.  Default:
      <literal>X25519MLKEM768:X25519:prime256v1</literal>.
      The hybrid group <literal>X25519MLKEM768</literal> combines classical
      X25519 with ML-KEM-768 for defense in depth.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><varname>ssl_pqc_sigalgs</varname> (<type>string</type>)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Colon-separated list of TLS signature algorithms.  Default: empty
      (use OpenSSL defaults).  Set to e.g.
      <literal>mldsa65:ed25519:rsa_pss_rsae_sha256</literal> for PQC
      certificate support.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   Client connection string example:
  </para>

<programlisting>
psql "host=localhost sslmode=require sslpqcgroups=X25519MLKEM768"
</programlisting>

  <para>
   The <structname>pg_stat_ssl</structname> view includes a
   <structfield>pqc_group</structfield> column that shows the negotiated
   key exchange group for each connection.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pqc-auth">
  <title>PQC Authentication</title>

  <para>
   FortressQL adds two new authentication methods in
   <filename>pg_hba.conf</filename>:
  </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>pqc-cert</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Certificate authentication that verifies the client certificate uses
      a PQC signature algorithm (ML-DSA or SLH-DSA).  Requires
      <literal>hostssl</literal> connection type.  Works like standard
      <literal>cert</literal> authentication but rejects certificates signed
      with classical algorithms.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>pqc-scram-sha-384</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      SCRAM authentication using SHA-384 instead of SHA-256, aligned with
      NIST Level 3 security.  The SASL mechanism names are
      <literal>SCRAM-SHA-384</literal> and
      <literal>SCRAM-SHA-384-PLUS</literal> (with channel binding).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   Example <filename>pg_hba.conf</filename> entries:
  </para>

<programlisting>
# PQC certificate authentication
hostssl all all 0.0.0.0/0 pqc-cert

# SCRAM-SHA-384 authentication
hostssl all all 0.0.0.0/0 pqc-scram-sha-384
</programlisting>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pqc-pgcrypto">
  <title>pgcrypto_pqc Extension</title>

  <para>
   The <literal>pgcrypto_pqc</literal> extension provides SQL-callable
   functions for PQC key encapsulation, digital signatures, and hybrid
   encryption.
  </para>

<programlisting>
CREATE EXTENSION pgcrypto_pqc;
</programlisting>

  <sect2 id="pqc-pgcrypto-kem">
   <title>Key Encapsulation (ML-KEM)</title>

<programlisting>
-- Generate a KEM keypair
SELECT * FROM pqc_kem_keygen('ML-KEM-768');

-- Encapsulate a shared secret
SELECT * FROM pqc_kem_encapsulate(public_key, 'ML-KEM-768');

-- Decapsulate to recover the shared secret
SELECT pqc_kem_decapsulate(ciphertext, secret_key, 'ML-KEM-768');
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pqc-pgcrypto-sig">
   <title>Digital Signatures (ML-DSA, SLH-DSA)</title>

<programlisting>
-- Generate a signature keypair
SELECT * FROM pqc_sig_keygen('ML-DSA-65');

-- Sign a message
SELECT pqc_sign('message'::bytea, secret_key, 'ML-DSA-65');

-- Verify a signature
SELECT pqc_verify('message'::bytea, signature, public_key, 'ML-DSA-65');
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pqc-pgcrypto-hybrid">
   <title>Hybrid Encryption (ML-KEM + AES-256-GCM)</title>

<programlisting>
-- Encrypt data (returns encrypted_data, encapsulated_key, public_key, secret_key)
SELECT * FROM pqc_encrypt('sensitive data'::bytea, 'ML-KEM-768');

-- Decrypt data
SELECT pqc_decrypt(encrypted_data, encapsulated_key, secret_key, 'ML-KEM-768');
</programlisting>

   <para>
    Hybrid encryption uses ML-KEM to encapsulate a shared secret, derives
    an AES-256 key via HKDF-SHA-256, then encrypts the data with
    AES-256-GCM.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pqc-pgcrypto-algorithms">
   <title>Algorithm Discovery</title>

<programlisting>
-- List all available PQC algorithms
SELECT * FROM pqc_algorithms();
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pqc-replication">
  <title>PQC Cluster Communication</title>

  <para>
   Streaming replication uses libpq for connections, so the PQC TLS
   configuration applies automatically.  Configure
   <varname>primary_conninfo</varname> in <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>
   on the standby:
  </para>

<programlisting>
primary_conninfo = 'host=primary sslmode=verify-full sslpqcmode=hybrid sslpqcgroups=X25519MLKEM768'
</programlisting>
 </sect1>
</chapter>
